From 9dc3ff2e63165617667acd87d3d07b19cc346543 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 20:11:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 71/71] resolve: a glob re-export keeps each item's own
 visibility, and imports may chain

Two related problems, seen compiling simd-json 0.18.1 against
value-trait 0.12.2:

    src/value/borrowed/serialize.rs:10:1 BUG: src/resolve/common.cpp:821:
    Recursive namespace import in HIR: ::"value_trait"::std pointed to ::"std-0_0_0"

value-trait's prelude does `pub use crate::*;`. The wildcard expansion
copied every item visible from the prelude with the glob's own `pub`
visibility, including the crate's implicit (private) `extern crate
std`, so `value_trait::prelude::std` was exported. simd-json's
`pub use value_trait::prelude::*;` then offered a `std` through its own
prelude, and `use std::io::Write` found it before the extern prelude.
rustc gives a glob re-export the minimum of the glob's and the item's
visibility, so a private item is imported for local use only: do the
same (a non-`pub` glob keeps its own visibility).

Independently, the resolver asserted that an HIR import never points at
another import. A glob re-export of a re-export (or of a legitimately
`pub extern crate x;`) records the intermediate path, so follow a chain
of imports to the final item instead, in both the type and the value
namespace. The wildcard indexer had the same one-hop assumption when
expanding a glob over an extern crate's module (a TODO for types, a bare
`throw` for values), and so did binding a `use` of such an item from
an extern crate ("no Import should refer to another"); all three follow
the chain now, with a chain ending at a crate root bound as that crate.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
